 "batch_notes": "(1) Std-container-as-model convention: I classified std BTreeMap/HashMap/VecDeque/Vec/BTreeSet used as a behavioural reference for a container (redb range, roaring machine, all rpds model tests) as model-oracle/oracle:model, and std used as the definitional semantics of an ordering/comparison (redb tuple Key::compare vs Ord, ropey ==/Ord/Hash vs String) as external-oracle; the rubric lists 'the language's stdlib' under external-oracle, so the two readings should be reconciled globally. (2) Recurring shape the rubric handles only via precedent: 'test/std writes the text, library parses it, expected value known by construction' (rhai int/float/string literal tests, ron untyped integer, roxmltree text/names/namespace). Following earlier batches these went to spec-postcondition even when named *_roundtrip. (3) roaring 'iterator oracle' properties compare a method with a naive computation over the crate's own iterator; classified internal-consistency (fast vs slow path) rather than model-oracle since the ground truth comes from the library. (4) Persistence properties in rpds (old versions unchanged after deriving new ones) fit no category cleanly; classified spec-postcondition with doc-contract. (5) Two attribution-less bugs (rkyv/1 deep recursion, rpds/2 degree 1) sit just outside a property's generator range (depth cap 150, degree list starting at 2); these caps may be silent gates around the bug but nothing in the batch says so, so they were marked none rather than inferred. (6) Quality: ron/1 has kind 'hang' but is a value-fidelity bug; roaring/1 is the only fixed bug in the batch. ropey byte_slices_count_lines_like_char_slices is a general property with a boosted generator and a KNOWN FAILURE marker, tagged pin; it appears narrowed from slices_behave_like_ropes_of_their_text which by its own doc also covers CRLF-splitting slices and so presumably gates somewhere not visible in the first 45 lines. (7) rpds bug rpds/1 (bits=64 shift overflow) is attributed directly to hegel_vector_new_with_bits_no_panic, whose sibling model property draws bits only 1..8 - same generator-range pattern as (5) but here the un-narrowed property exists."
